title: Built-in variables
tags: TW5Syntax GrammarData
caption: Built-in variables

A variable an author writes may come from TiddlyWiki's core or from their own `\define`. A reader
benefits from telling the two apart, and a regular expression cannot: the name alone carries no
mark. So the grammar holds a list, and this wiki holds the list.

! What stands here

The data tiddler <<lar-link "$:/tw5-syntax/BuiltInVariables">> carries two fields:

|!Field |!Meaning |
|`exact` |a name the core defines, matched whole |
|`prefixes` |a family the core reserves, matched by its opening |

The seven exact names come from the tiddlers TiddlyWiki's own documentation tags ''Core
Variables''. The `tv-` prefix covers the transclusion family — fifteen names at the time of
writing, and the core adds to it — so the prefix carries them without a list that goes stale
between releases.

! How the grammar reads it

`tools/builtins-sync.js` reads this tiddler and writes the alternation the grammar matches on.
Run it with `--check` and it reports drift without writing; a gate runs it that way, so the
grammar and this tiddler cannot part company without the gate saying so.

Edit the list here. Run `npm run builtins -- --write`. The grammar follows.

! Why a bare minimum

Every name added here claims a construct the core owns. A name that turns out to belong to an
author paints their variable as the language's, which reads as a lie about whose thing it is.
The list stays short on purpose, and the wiki keeps it where an operator can weigh an addition
against what the core actually documents.
